      "updated": 1793860582751,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Subqueries in SQL\nA subquery is a query inside another SQL query.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Subqueries in SQL\nA subquery is a query inside another SQL query.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "I0BSu-fqkvp-P4CVx7Bjg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1551.7724967778709,
      "y": -1322.5277777777776,
      "width": 1822.7127415069695,
      "height": 85.15473887814306,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1921920508,
      "version": 617,
      "versionNonce": 1461224831,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793860104938,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is GROUP BY in SQL?\nGROUP BY groups rows that have the same value in a particular column, allowing us to perform calculations on each group using functions like\nCOUNT(), SUM(), AVG(), MAX(), and MIN().",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is GROUP BY in SQL?\nGROUP BY groups rows that have the same value in a particular column, allowing us to perform calculations on each group using functions like COUNT(), SUM(), AVG(), MAX(), and MIN().",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "kt9dfVd2FOUZSzojwlq_s",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1566.8458558083216,
      "y": -546.8109274800086,
      "width": 1832.050196520945,
      "height": 198.69439071566714,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 739239620,
      "version": 758,
      "versionNonce": 839482783,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793860104938,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "GROUP_CONCAT() in SQL\n\nGROUP_CONCAT() is an aggregate function in MySQL that combines multiple values from different rows into a single string, separated by commas\nby default. It is particularly useful when working with many-to-many relationships. For example, a book can belong to multiple categories,\nand a category can contain multiple books. To represent this relationship, we use a junction table containing book_id and category_id. When\nwe join the three tables, a book appears multiple times if it belongs to multiple categories. Using GROUP_CONCAT(), we can combine all the\ncategory names into a single column, so each book appears only once in the final result.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "GROUP_CONCAT() in SQL\n\nGROUP_CONCAT() is an aggregate function in MySQL that combines multiple values from different rows into a single string, separated by commas by default. It is particularly useful when working with many-to-many relationships. For example, a book can belong to multiple categories, and a category can contain multiple books. To represent this relationship, we use a junction table containing book_id and category_id. When we join the three tables, a book appears multiple times if it belongs to multiple categories. Using GROUP_CONCAT(), we can combine all the category names into a single column, so each book appears only once in the final result.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "PGDYdc4vAbM7FIP8gmmGJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 828.0555555555566,
      "y": 1716.1614043918207,
      "width": 1836.1666666666665,
      "height": 369.00386847195324,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3K",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1047995004,
      "version": 1258,
      "versionNonce": 998121119,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793860491851,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When working with large databases, reducing the number of rows involved in a `JOIN` operation can improve query performance because joining\nlarge datasets can require significant processing resources.\n\nIn the first query, we perform a `LEFT JOIN` between the `customers` and `orders` tables and then apply a filter to retrieve only customers\nfrom New York using the `WHERE` clause. Conceptually, this approach involves joining the tables before filtering the results.\n\nIn the second query, we first filter the `customers` table to retrieve only customers from New York using a subquery. We then perform a `LEFT\nJOIN` between this smaller result set and the `orders` table. Since fewer customers need to participate in the join, this approach can reduce\nthe amount of data processed and improve performance, especially when the original `customers` table contains millions of rows.\n\nHowever, this does not mean the second query will always be faster. Modern relational databases, such as PostgreSQL and MySQL, have query\noptimizers that can recognize equivalent queries and choose efficient execution plans automatically. Therefore, the actual performance\nimprovement depends on factors such as indexes, table sizes, data distribution, and the execution plan selected by the database.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When working with large databases, reducing the number of rows involved in a `JOIN` operation can improve query performance because joining large datasets can require significant processing resources.\n\nIn the first query, we perform a `LEFT JOIN` between the `customers` and `orders` tables and then apply a filter to retrieve only customers from New York using the `WHERE` clause. Conceptually, this approach involves joining the tables before filtering the results.\n\nIn the second query, we first filter the `customers` table to retrieve only customers from New York using a subquery. We then perform a `LEFT JOIN` between this smaller result set and the `orders` table. Since fewer customers need to participate in the join, this approach can reduce the amount of data processed and improve performance, especially when the original `customers` table contains millions of rows.\n\nHowever, this does not mean the second query will always be faster. Modern relational databases, such as PostgreSQL and MySQL, have query optimizers that can recognize equivalent queries and choose efficient execution plans automatically. Therefore, the actual performance improvement depends on factors such as indexes, table sizes, data distribution, and the execution plan selected by the database.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1_LVBpY3ngxJoCm9_VdYq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 848.38888888889,
      "y": 899.1583340959669,
      "width": 1296.166666666666,
      "height": 170.30947775628613,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2054410360,
      "version": 1693,
      "versionNonce": 732110399,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "w4ntCGVI-msKNGgPNHpmU",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1793860554041,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. UNION\n\nUNION is used to combine the results of two or more SELECT queries into a single result set. It\nautomatically removes duplicate rows, meaning that if every column's value in one row matches the\ncorresponding values in another row, only one copy of that row is returned. Both queries must\nreturn the same number of columns, and their corresponding columns must have compatible data types.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. UNION\n\nUNION is used to combine the results of two or more SELECT queries into a single result set. It automatically removes duplicate rows, meaning that if every column's value in one row matches the corresponding values in another row, only one copy of that row is returned. Both queries must return the same number of columns, and their corresponding columns must have compatible data types.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "y86thVtqCT6RUqiEq0k3Q",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4547.466312977479,
      "y": -1507.9937214092458,
      "width": 1437.7205203314104,
      "height": 681.2379110251445,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3LG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 705069176,
      "version": 1967,
      "versionNonce": 1528561951,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "R-ysjYvHCEi5vSo6Yghm8",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1793860668352,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An alias in SQL is a temporary name given to a column, table, or expression to make a query easier to read and\nunderstand. \n\nWe use the AS keyword to assign an alias to a column. For example :- \nSELECT salary AS s FROM Employees;\ndisplays the salary column with the name s in the query result. \n\nWe can also create aliases for calculated values, such as :- \nSELECT salary, (salary * 1.1) AS newSalary FROM Employees;\nwhich returns the original salary along with a new column named `newSalary` containing the salary increased by\n10%. \n\nSimilarly, we can assign aliases to tables to make queries shorter and easier to write. For example :- \nSELECT e.id, e.salary FROM Employees AS e;\nassigns the alias `e` to the `Employees` table, allowing us to access its columns using `e.id` and `e.salary`.\n\nAliases are particularly useful when joining multiple tables because they help distinguish columns belonging\nto different tables. \n\nOne important thing to remember is that the `AS` keyword is optional, so `SELECT salary s FROM Employees;` and\n`SELECT salary AS s FROM Employees;` both work, and `FROM Employees e` is equivalent to `FROM Employees AS e`.\nColumn aliases generally affect how columns are displayed in the query result, while table aliases are used to\nreference tables within the query. These aliases are temporary and do not change the actual column names or\ntable names in the database.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An alias in SQL is a temporary name given to a column, table, or expression to make a query easier to read and understand. \n\nWe use the AS keyword to assign an alias to a column. For example :- \nSELECT salary AS s FROM Employees;\ndisplays the salary column with the name s in the query result. \n\nWe can also create aliases for calculated values, such as :- \nSELECT salary, (salary * 1.1) AS newSalary FROM Employees;\nwhich returns the original salary along with a new column named `newSalary` containing the salary increased by 10%. \n\nSimilarly, we can assign aliases to tables to make queries shorter and easier to write. For example :- \nSELECT e.id, e.salary FROM Employees AS e;\nassigns the alias `e` to the `Employees` table, allowing us to access its columns using `e.id` and `e.salary`. \n\nAliases are particularly useful when joining multiple tables because they help distinguish columns belonging to different tables. \n\nOne important thing to remember is that the `AS` keyword is optional, so `SELECT salary s FROM Employees;` and `SELECT salary AS s FROM Employees;` both work, and `FROM Employees e` is equivalent to `FROM Employees AS e`. Column aliases generally affect how columns are displayed in the query result, while table aliases are used to reference tables within the query. These aliases are temporary and do not change the actual column names or table names in the database.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3enOhpOlDbSk8-S5TUVmH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 854.38888888889,
      "y": 1089.158334095967,
      "width": 1296.166666666666,
      "height": 141.9245647969051,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3LV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1348268552,
      "version": 1753,
      "versionNonce": 1717546367,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793860501615,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. UNION ALL\n\nUNION ALL also combines the results of two or more SELECT queries, but it keeps duplicate rows\ninstead of removing them. Since it does not need to eliminate duplicates, it is generally faster\nthan UNION.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. UNION ALL\n\nUNION ALL also combines the results of two or more SELECT queries, but it keeps duplicate rows instead of removing them. Since it does not need to eliminate duplicates, it is generally faster than UNION.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "CpaacBfSKl0MVjToJR8UB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1420.2777777777774,
      "y": 1052.0837720586742,
      "width": 877.0000000000005,
      "height": 227.07930367504815,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1769198374,
      "version": 1796,
      "versionNonce": 739674001,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793860473005,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. LEFT JOIN\n\nA LEFT JOIN returns all the records from the left table\n(customers), regardless of whether they have a matching record in\nthe right table (orders). If a customer has placed an order, the\nmatching order information is included in the result. If a customer\nhas not placed any orders, the order-related columns contain NULL\nvalues.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. LEFT JOIN\n\nA LEFT JOIN returns all the records from the left table (customers), regardless of whether they have a matching record in the right table (orders). If a customer has placed an order, the matching order information is included in the result. If a customer has not placed any orders, the order-related columns contain NULL values.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8-CNxWX2vawx94g7AFd6Z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1415.2777777777774,
