    {
      "id": "8t0-dd8vPpahoqFLthmDt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 496.88334564258776,
      "y": -1362.6848252893683,
      "width": 1792.7205203314104,
      "height": 936.7021276595736,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aX",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 895136401,
      "version": 2412,
      "versionNonce": 107551729,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793860156249,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n1. CONCAT(): Combines multiple strings or column values into a single string.\n   SELECT CONCAT(name, ' ', lastname) AS fullName FROM data;\n\n2. SUBSTRING(): Extracts a portion of a string. `SUBSTRING(name, 1, 3)` returns 3 characters starting from position 1.\n   SELECT name, SUBSTRING(name, 1, 3) AS subNames FROM data;\n\n3. UPPER(): Converts all characters in a string to uppercase.\n   SELECT UPPER(name) FROM data;\n\n4. LOCATE(): Returns the position of a substring within a string. It returns `0` if the substring is not found.\n   SELECT name, LOCATE('s', name) FROM data;\n\n5. REPLACE(): Replaces all occurrences of a specified substring with another string.\n   SELECT REPLACE(name, 'old', 'new') FROM data;\n\n6. REVERSE(): Reverses the order of characters in a string.\n   SELECT REVERSE(name) FROM data;\n\n7. ASCII(): Returns the ASCII value of the first character in a string.\n   SELECT ASCII('A');\n\n8. FIELD(): Returns the position of a value in a specified list. It can be used with `ORDER BY` to sort results in a custom order.\n   SELECT * FROM products\n   ORDER BY FIELD(category, 'Electronics', 'Appliances', 'Furniture');\n   Here, `Electronics` gets position 1, `Appliances` gets position 2, and `Furniture` gets position 3. Rows are sorted according to these\npositions.\n\n9. SOUNDEX(): Returns a phonetic code representing how a string sounds. Similar-sounding names may produce the same code, making it\nuseful for finding names with similar pronunciations.\n   SELECT *\n   FROM peoples\n   WHERE SOUNDEX(first_name) = SOUNDEX('John');",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n1. CONCAT(): Combines multiple strings or column values into a single string.\n   SELECT CONCAT(name, ' ', lastname) AS fullName FROM data;\n\n2. SUBSTRING(): Extracts a portion of a string. `SUBSTRING(name, 1, 3)` returns 3 characters starting from position 1.\n   SELECT name, SUBSTRING(name, 1, 3) AS subNames FROM data;\n\n3. UPPER(): Converts all characters in a string to uppercase.\n   SELECT UPPER(name) FROM data;\n\n4. LOCATE(): Returns the position of a substring within a string. It returns `0` if the substring is not found.\n   SELECT name, LOCATE('s', name) FROM data;\n\n5. REPLACE(): Replaces all occurrences of a specified substring with another string.\n   SELECT REPLACE(name, 'old', 'new') FROM data;\n\n6. REVERSE(): Reverses the order of characters in a string.\n   SELECT REVERSE(name) FROM data;\n\n7. ASCII(): Returns the ASCII value of the first character in a string.\n   SELECT ASCII('A');\n\n8. FIELD(): Returns the position of a value in a specified list. It can be used with `ORDER BY` to sort results in a custom order.\n   SELECT * FROM products\n   ORDER BY FIELD(category, 'Electronics', 'Appliances', 'Furniture');\n   Here, `Electronics` gets position 1, `Appliances` gets position 2, and `Furniture` gets position 3. Rows are sorted according to these positions.\n\n9. SOUNDEX(): Returns a phonetic code representing how a string sounds. Similar-sounding names may produce the same code, making it useful for finding names with similar pronunciations.\n   SELECT *\n   FROM peoples\n   WHERE SOUNDEX(first_name) = SOUNDEX('John');",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
