    {
      "id": "0L82G17rw7PYktoGuOYRI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2478.1611043644107,
      "y": -1379.7468140561969,
      "width": 850.2864584406041,
      "height": 1050.2417794970977,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ae",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 989258719,
      "version": 2648,
      "versionNonce": 1505763775,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "Sn4vmZJWhNpufmO0HapEP",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1793860521479,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The standard date format in SQL is `YYYY-MM-DD`.\n\n1. Current Date and Time Functions\n\n- NOW(): Returns the current date and time.\n  Example: `SELECT NOW();`\n- CURDATE(): Returns the current date.\n  Example: `SELECT CURDATE();`\n- CURTIME(): Returns the current time.\n  Example: `SELECT CURTIME();`\n\n2. Extracting Date and Time Parts\n\n- YEAR(): Extracts the year from a date.\n- MONTH(): Extracts the month number from a date.\n- DAY(): Extracts the day of the month.\n- HOUR(): Extracts the hour from a time.\n- MINUTE(): Extracts the minute from a time.\n- SECOND(): Extracts the second from a time.\n\nExample: \nSELECT YEAR(NOW()), MONTH(NOW()), DAY(NOW()); SELECT HOUR(NOW()),\nMINUTE(NOW()), SECOND(NOW());\n\n3. DATE_FORMAT()\nDATE_FORMAT() converts a date into a specified format.\nExample: SELECT DATE_FORMAT('2025-03-13', '%W, %M %e, %Y');\n\nOutput: `Thursday, March 13, 2025`\n\nCommon format specifiers:\n- `%W` — Full weekday name\n- `%M` — Full month name\n- `%e` — Day of the month (1–31)\n- `%Y` — Four-digit year\n- `%m` — Month number (01–12)\n- `%d` — Day of the month (01–31)",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The standard date format in SQL is `YYYY-MM-DD`.\n\n1. Current Date and Time Functions\n\n- NOW(): Returns the current date and time.\n  Example: `SELECT NOW();`\n- CURDATE(): Returns the current date.\n  Example: `SELECT CURDATE();`\n- CURTIME(): Returns the current time.\n  Example: `SELECT CURTIME();`\n\n2. Extracting Date and Time Parts\n\n- YEAR(): Extracts the year from a date.\n- MONTH(): Extracts the month number from a date.\n- DAY(): Extracts the day of the month.\n- HOUR(): Extracts the hour from a time.\n- MINUTE(): Extracts the minute from a time.\n- SECOND(): Extracts the second from a time.\n\nExample: \nSELECT YEAR(NOW()), MONTH(NOW()), DAY(NOW()); SELECT HOUR(NOW()), MINUTE(NOW()), SECOND(NOW());\n\n3. DATE_FORMAT()\nDATE_FORMAT() converts a date into a specified format.\nExample: SELECT DATE_FORMAT('2025-03-13', '%W, %M %e, %Y');\n\nOutput: `Thursday, March 13, 2025`\n\nCommon format specifiers:\n- `%W` — Full weekday name\n- `%M` — Full month name\n- `%e` — Day of the month (1–31)\n- `%Y` — Four-digit year\n- `%m` — Month number (01–12)\n- `%d` — Day of the month (01–31)",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "R0fXBVE7Y2Q2vEq0bnjUl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3926.8388956355893,
      "y": -37.73168632866464,
      "width": 1541.6437812095633,
      "height": 1220.5512572533837,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ael",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1784564587,
      "version": 3160,
      "versionNonce": 185451084,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "gFxZtgfjE14nRhAASRs0L",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794501067070,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We choose a database based on our application's requirements, data structure, relationships, consistency requirements,\nquery patterns, and scalability needs.\n\nWhen should we consider SQL databases?\n\nIf our data has a well-defined structure, there are relationships between different datasets, we need complex queries,\nor our application requires strong ACID transaction guarantees, we should consider SQL databases such as PostgreSQL\nand MySQL.\n\nHowever, this does not mean NoSQL databases cannot provide these features. Some NoSQL databases support ACID\ntransactions, relationships or relationship-like data models, complex queries, and horizontal scaling. The actual\ncapabilities depend on the specific database and how it is designed.\n\nWhen should we consider NoSQL databases?\n\nIf our application needs to store flexible data, documents in JSON-like formats, or key-value pairs, NoSQL databases\nmight be a good choice. Some NoSQL databases are also designed to handle high write throughput and distribute data\nacross multiple servers.\n\nHowever, this does not mean SQL databases cannot handle flexible data or high write throughput. We must evaluate the\ncapabilities of the specific database and our application's requirements before making a decision.\n\nExample: Storing analytics events\n\nSuppose we are building an analytics service that records different user activities, such as logging in, liking a\npost, or watching a video. Each event might contain different information.\n\nWe could use a traditional SQL table with columns for all possible event fields. When an event occurs, we fill in the\nrelevant columns and leave the others as NULL. However, if we have many different types of events, this approach might\nresult in numerous columns containing NULL values.\n\nAlternatively, modern SQL databases such as PostgreSQL support JSON data types, allowing us to store event-specific\ninformation in a JSON column without creating a separate column for every possible field.\n\nWe could also use MongoDB, which allows documents in the same collection to contain different fields naturally.\n\nTherefore, both SQL and NoSQL databases can handle analytics events. The decision depends on factors such as the\nvolume of incoming events, the flexibility of the data, the required queries, the storage model, and the scalability\nrequirements.\n\nFinal takeaway: We should not choose a database simply because our data is structured or unstructured, or because we\nexpect high write throughput. Instead, we should understand our application's requirements and compare the\ncapabilities of different databases before choosing one.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We choose a database based on our application's requirements, data structure, relationships, consistency requirements, query patterns, and scalability needs.\n\nWhen should we consider SQL databases?\n\nIf our data has a well-defined structure, there are relationships between different datasets, we need complex queries, or our application requires strong ACID transaction guarantees, we should consider SQL databases such as PostgreSQL and MySQL.\n\nHowever, this does not mean NoSQL databases cannot provide these features. Some NoSQL databases support ACID transactions, relationships or relationship-like data models, complex queries, and horizontal scaling. The actual capabilities depend on the specific database and how it is designed.\n\nWhen should we consider NoSQL databases?\n\nIf our application needs to store flexible data, documents in JSON-like formats, or key-value pairs, NoSQL databases might be a good choice. Some NoSQL databases are also designed to handle high write throughput and distribute data across multiple servers.\n\nHowever, this does not mean SQL databases cannot handle flexible data or high write throughput. We must evaluate the capabilities of the specific database and our application's requirements before making a decision.\n\nExample: Storing analytics events\n\nSuppose we are building an analytics service that records different user activities, such as logging in, liking a post, or watching a video. Each event might contain different information.\n\nWe could use a traditional SQL table with columns for all possible event fields. When an event occurs, we fill in the relevant columns and leave the others as NULL. However, if we have many different types of events, this approach might result in numerous columns containing NULL values.\n\nAlternatively, modern SQL databases such as PostgreSQL support JSON data types, allowing us to store event-specific information in a JSON column without creating a separate column for every possible field.\n\nWe could also use MongoDB, which allows documents in the same collection to contain different fields naturally.\n\nTherefore, both SQL and NoSQL databases can handle analytics events. The decision depends on factors such as the volume of incoming events, the flexibility of the data, the required queries, the storage model, and the scalability requirements.\n\nFinal takeaway: We should not choose a database simply because our data is structured or unstructured, or because we expect high write throughput. Instead, we should understand our application's requirements and compare the capabilities of different databases before choosing one.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "0iNm3Uxq6TfFIupFpZ1Y8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3956.2896604780467,
      "y": 1581.1524757940826,
      "width": 1541.6437812095633,
      "height": 851.5473887814305,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aep",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1842317361,
      "version": 3383,
      "versionNonce": 1276181492,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "JJCMqu-ZPfgDP4s5OXhdi",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794501062665,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Suppose we execute: SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'shivam@gmail.com';\n\nThe database has to figure out how to execute this query efficiently.\n\nFor example:\n- Should it scan the entire users table?\n- Should it use an index on the email column?\n- Which execution strategy should it choose?\n\nThe database's query optimizer decides this and creates an Execution Plan. An execution plan is basically a set of\ninstructions that tells the database how to execute the query. for e.g. SQL database engine make this instructions :- \n\nExecution Plan\n1. Use the email index.\n2. Find the matching row.\n3. Retrieve the required columns.\n\nNow what is Execution Plan Caching?\n\nSuppose we execute the same query 1,000 times. The database might need to parse and optimize the query repeatedly,\ndepending on its execution strategy. But creating an execution plan also takes resources. So some database systems\ncache execution plans or prepared statement information so that they can reuse previously prepared work instead of\nrepeating everything.\n\nFor example:\n1. First execution: Database parses the query and creates an execution plan.\n2. Second execution: Database may reuse the cached plan.\n3. Third execution: Database may reuse the same plan again.\n\nThis can save some CPU and processing time.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Suppose we execute: SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'shivam@gmail.com';\n\nThe database has to figure out how to execute this query efficiently.\n\nFor example:\n- Should it scan the entire users table?\n- Should it use an index on the email column?\n- Which execution strategy should it choose?\n\nThe database's query optimizer decides this and creates an Execution Plan. An execution plan is basically a set of instructions that tells the database how to execute the query. for e.g. SQL database engine make this instructions :- \n\nExecution Plan\n1. Use the email index.\n2. Find the matching row.\n3. Retrieve the required columns.\n\nNow what is Execution Plan Caching?\n\nSuppose we execute the same query 1,000 times. The database might need to parse and optimize the query repeatedly, depending on its execution strategy. But creating an execution plan also takes resources. So some database systems cache execution plans or prepared statement information so that they can reuse previously prepared work instead of repeating everything.\n\nFor example:\n1. First execution: Database parses the query and creates an execution plan.\n2. Second execution: Database may reuse the cached plan.\n3. Third execution: Database may reuse the same plan again.\n\nThis can save some CPU and processing time.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "AoFSzbGLB5RBROoWn25W-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 278.0420567453633,
      "y": 2967.268313671335,
      "width": 1541.6437812095633,
      "height": 56.76982591876204,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aet",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1962345626,
      "version": 3219,
      "versionNonce": 1970746458,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793810184284,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A View is basically a virtual table created using an SQL query. It allows us to show only the columns and rows that we\nwant without giving direct access to the original table.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A View is basically a virtual table created using an SQL query. It allows us to show only the columns and rows that we want without giving direct access to the original table.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hFNjNWYAUkNsRqTEHKslj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1539.4579432546366,
      "y": 3987.1881915178874,
      "width": 1541.6437812095633,
      "height": 425.77369439071526,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aev",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1671904922,
      "version": 3396,
      "versionNonce": 1896897554,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793898362967,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Now, this is where Views become useful for security purposes.\n\nSuppose we have a users table containing sensitive information such as passwords, salary, and personal details.\n\nWe don't want our customer support team to access all these columns.\n\nSo, instead of giving them direct access to the original table, we can:\n1. Create a View containing only the columns they need.\n2. Give them SELECT permission on that View.\n3. Avoid giving them direct access to the original table.\nThis way, they can access only the information we want them to see.\n\nOne important thing: A View is not automatically a security mechanism. We must configure permissions correctly and\nensure the database's View security rules are understood. Also, passwords should never be stored as plain text; they\nshould be securely hashed.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now, this is where Views become useful for security purposes.\n\nSuppose we have a users table containing sensitive information such as passwords, salary, and personal details.\n\nWe don't want our customer support team to access all these columns.\n\nSo, instead of giving them direct access to the original table, we can:\n1. Create a View containing only the columns they need.\n2. Give them SELECT permission on that View.\n3. Avoid giving them direct access to the original table.\nThis way, they can access only the information we want them to see.\n\nOne important thing: A View is not automatically a security mechanism. We must configure permissions correctly and ensure the database's View security rules are understood. Also, passwords should never be stored as plain text; they should be securely hashed.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "a3l6NuLPB8FT6PVt0hhHe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1985.6990864523527,
      "y": 2924.635802845329,
      "width": 1117.4792958349426,
      "height": 113.53965183752408,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aew",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 43574874,
      "version": 3618,
      "versionNonce": 2144471942,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793810970748,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A function in SQL is similar to a function in programming languages like Java or\nJavaScript. It allows us to write reusable logic that can accept parameters, perform\noperations, and return a result.\nNow we can call it: SELECT get_total(10, 20);",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A function in SQL is similar to a function in programming languages like Java or JavaScript. It allows us to write reusable logic that can accept parameters, perform operations, and return a result.\nNow we can call it: SELECT get_total(10, 20);",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "f6HY47xycippTDgJTTfVr",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2589.1691861887225,
      "y": 4632.140076078687,
      "width": 1117.4792958349426,
      "height": 56.76982591876204,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aewV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 260594074,
      "version": 4242,
      "versionNonce": 1676598257,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1794678223991,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A Stored Procedure is a group of SQL statements saved inside the database that we can\nexecute whenever required.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A Stored Procedure is a group of SQL statements saved inside the database that we can execute whenever required.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
