      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Now, suppose we want to show only the user_id, name, and email columns to a\nparticular user. Instead of giving access to the complete table, we can create a\nView.\n\nNow whenever we execute: SELECT * FROM public_users;\n\nWe will only get the columns defined in that View",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now, suppose we want to show only the user_id, name, and email columns to a particular user. Instead of giving access to the complete table, we can create a View.\n\nNow whenever we execute: SELECT * FROM public_users;\n\nWe will only get the columns defined in that View",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "loKrkH7f9IoRrBsiemD5I",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 274.7087234120298,
      "y": 3520.601647004668,
      "width": 1549.9771145428963,
      "height": 198.69439071566714,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aez",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1986036506,
      "version": 3530,
      "versionNonce": 1696949722,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793810184284,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Important points:\n- A View generally does not store the actual data separately. It stores the SQL query definition and retrieves data\nfrom the underlying tables when queried.\n- We can also use WHERE, JOIN, and other SQL operations while creating Views.\n- We can create multiple Views over the same table for different purposes.\n- Some databases also support Materialized Views, which physically store the query results and need to be refreshed to\nreflect changes.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Important points:\n- A View generally does not store the actual data separately. It stores the SQL query definition and retrieves data from the underlying tables when queried.\n- We can also use WHERE, JOIN, and other SQL operations while creating Views.\n- We can create multiple Views over the same table for different purposes.\n- Some databases also support Materialized Views, which physically store the query results and need to be refreshed to reflect changes.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "6xHv8atscrGz3yxUktw5q",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3428.993013815467,
      "y": -1500.8563927176867,
      "width": 850.2864584406041,
      "height": 1135.3965183752407,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "af",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2060141055,
      "version": 2704,
      "versionNonce": 1499711999,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "XtOzyBp6wt8XtuLEIkTqD",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1793860537504,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "4. DATE_ADD()\n\nDATE_ADD() adds a specified time interval to a date.\nExample: \nSELECT DATE_ADD('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 7 DAY);\n\nOutput: `2025-03-20`\n\nWe can also add months or years: \nSELECT DATE_ADD('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 2 MONTH); \nSELECT DATE_ADD('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 1 YEAR);\n\n5. DATE_SUB()\n\nDATE_SUB() subtracts a specified time interval from a date.\nExample:\nSELECT DATE_SUB('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 7 DAY);\n\nOutput: `2025-03-06`\n\nWe can also subtract months or years:\nSELECT DATE_SUB('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 2 MONTH);\nSELECT DATE_SUB('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 1 YEAR);\n\n6. DATEDIFF()\n\nDATEDIFF() returns the difference between two dates in days. It\ncalculates the first date minus the second date.\n\nExample:\nSELECT DATEDIFF('2025-03-15', '2025-03-13');\n\nOutput: `2`\n\nIf the first date is earlier than the second date, the result is\nnegative.\n\nSELECT DATEDIFF('2025-03-13', '2025-03-15');\n\nOutput: `-2`",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "4. DATE_ADD()\n\nDATE_ADD() adds a specified time interval to a date.\nExample: \nSELECT DATE_ADD('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 7 DAY);\n\nOutput: `2025-03-20`\n\nWe can also add months or years: \nSELECT DATE_ADD('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 2 MONTH); \nSELECT DATE_ADD('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 1 YEAR);\n\n5. DATE_SUB()\n\nDATE_SUB() subtracts a specified time interval from a date.\nExample:\nSELECT DATE_SUB('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 7 DAY);\n\nOutput: `2025-03-06`\n\nWe can also subtract months or years:\nSELECT DATE_SUB('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 2 MONTH);\nSELECT DATE_SUB('2025-03-13', INTERVAL 1 YEAR);\n\n6. DATEDIFF()\n\nDATEDIFF() returns the difference between two dates in days. It calculates the first date minus the second date.\n\nExample:\nSELECT DATEDIFF('2025-03-15', '2025-03-13');\n\nOutput: `2`\n\nIf the first date is earlier than the second date, the result is negative.\n\nSELECT DATEDIFF('2025-03-13', '2025-03-15');\n\nOutput: `-2`",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
