    {
      "id": "WakwTQHXgS8OzVT4S4Cf2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1978.999581002367,
      "y": 3062.4340157910888,
      "width": 1545.0045431539577,
      "height": 965.0870406189547,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aw",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 886849626,
      "version": 4278,
      "versionNonce": 682240593,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "X4Yrx65M4lXDbsI9rd3rx",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1793859862196,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Important properties of SQL Functions\nThere are different characteristics that we can define while creating functions. Some of them are:\n\n1. DETERMINISTIC\n\nIt means that whenever we provide the same input, the function always returns the same output.\nFor example: SELECT get_total(10, 20);\n\nIt will always return 30.\n\nA function that returns the current date or time is generally non-deterministic because its output can change even\nwhen its input remains the same.\n\n2. NOT DETERMINISTIC\n\nIt means the function is not guaranteed to return the same result for the same input. For example, a function using\nNOW() may return different results every time it is called.\n\n3. NO SQL\n\nIt indicates that the function does not contain SQL statements. For or example, a function that only performs\nmathematical calculations.\n\n4. CONTAINS SQL\n\nIt means the function contains SQL statements but does not read or modify database data.\n\n5. READS SQL DATA\n\nIt means the function contains SQL statements that read data from database tables but do not modify that data.\n\n6. MODIFIES SQL DATA\n\nIt means the function contains SQL statements that can modify database data, such as INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Important properties of SQL Functions\nThere are different characteristics that we can define while creating functions. Some of them are:\n\n1. DETERMINISTIC\n\nIt means that whenever we provide the same input, the function always returns the same output.\nFor example: SELECT get_total(10, 20);\n\nIt will always return 30.\n\nA function that returns the current date or time is generally non-deterministic because its output can change even when its input remains the same.\n\n2. NOT DETERMINISTIC\n\nIt means the function is not guaranteed to return the same result for the same input. For example, a function using NOW() may return different results every time it is called.\n\n3. NO SQL\n\nIt indicates that the function does not contain SQL statements. For or example, a function that only performs mathematical calculations.\n\n4. CONTAINS SQL\n\nIt means the function contains SQL statements but does not read or modify database data.\n\n5. READS SQL DATA\n\nIt means the function contains SQL statements that read data from database tables but do not modify that data.\n\n6. MODIFIES SQL DATA\n\nIt means the function contains SQL statements that can modify database data, such as INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "OZtlKURm7fpdx9uWzxqYU",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4391.8177628205485,
      "y": 4422.946229197158,
      "width": 1502.6961999486773,
      "height": 283.8491295938102,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "awV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 644937038,
      "version": 4798,
      "versionNonce": 1236003487,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "X4Yrx65M4lXDbsI9rd3rx",
          "type": "arrow"
        },
        {
          "id": "TRSYXgylTWLBZ6VuAKNSV",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678209960,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A Trigger is a database object that automatically executes a predefined set of SQL statements whenever a particular\nevent occurs on a table. These events are generally INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations (also called CUD\noperations).\n\nWe can decide whether we want our trigger to execute BEFORE or AFTER the operation.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a customers table, and whenever someone deletes a customer, we want to store that\ncustomer's information in another table called customer_archive so that we don't lose the deleted data.\n\nWe can create a trigger for this:",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A Trigger is a database object that automatically executes a predefined set of SQL statements whenever a particular event occurs on a table. These events are generally INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations (also called CUD operations).\n\nWe can decide whether we want our trigger to execute BEFORE or AFTER the operation.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a customers table, and whenever someone deletes a customer, we want to store that customer's information in another table called customer_archive so that we don't lose the deleted data.\n\nWe can create a trigger for this:",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ykDsgutQdV7HWajNj6vq4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3697.0274349771516,
      "y": 2870.369217497555,
      "width": 1091.7401589317958,
      "height": 340.61895551257226,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "awd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2045779406,
      "version": 5208,
      "versionNonce": 734252977,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "X4Yrx65M4lXDbsI9rd3rx",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678211422,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An Event in SQL is a task or set of SQL statements that the database automatically\nexecutes at a particular scheduled time or after a specific interval of time. It is\nvery similar to a Cron Job in Linux, where we schedule tasks to run automatically\nwithout manually executing them.\n\nEvents are useful for performing background database tasks such as deleting old\nrecords, generating reports, updating statistics, cleaning temporary data, and\narchiving old records.\n\nTypes of SQL Events :- \n\nA. One-time Scheduled Event",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An Event in SQL is a task or set of SQL statements that the database automatically executes at a particular scheduled time or after a specific interval of time. It is very similar to a Cron Job in Linux, where we schedule tasks to run automatically without manually executing them.\n\nEvents are useful for performing background database tasks such as deleting old records, generating reports, updating statistics, cleaning temporary data, and archiving old records.\n\nTypes of SQL Events :- \n\nA. One-time Scheduled Event",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "tb4EV2rGAjSMNL3W73oWp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3697.544582345423,
      "y": 3369.9214846105488,
      "width": 287.9910932259781,
      "height": 28.38491295938102,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "awh",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1346898898,
      "version": 5458,
      "versionNonce": 986214321,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "X4Yrx65M4lXDbsI9rd3rx",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678166437,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "B. Recurring Event",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "B. Recurring Event",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "frYx4uvWInOuELll3aY14",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4393.382490651753,
      "y": 5014.220796490434,
      "width": 1510.209938773726,
      "height": 454.1586073500963,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "awl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 161918030,
      "version": 4944,
      "versionNonce": 53690047,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "X4Yrx65M4lXDbsI9rd3rx",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678209960,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "FOR EACH ROW\n\nThis is important to understand. FOR EACH ROW means that the trigger will execute separately for every row affected\nby the operation. For example, suppose our customers table has 100 customers, and we execute: DELETE FROM customers\nWHERE city = 'Delhi';\nSuppose 10 customers belong to Delhi. Now, because we have used FOR EACH ROW, our trigger will execute 10 times,\nonce for every customer being deleted. This is also called a Row-Level Trigger. In MySQL, triggers are row-level\ntriggers, so FOR EACH ROW is required when creating them.\n\nOLD and NEW\n\nOLD and NEW are special references available inside triggers. They allow us to access the values of the row before\nand after an operation. Think of them as references to the row's previous and new values.\n\nOLD: Represents the existing row values before an operation.\nNEW: Represents the new row values being inserted or the updated values after an update.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "FOR EACH ROW\n\nThis is important to understand. FOR EACH ROW means that the trigger will execute separately for every row affected by the operation. For example, suppose our customers table has 100 customers, and we execute: DELETE FROM customers WHERE city = 'Delhi';\nSuppose 10 customers belong to Delhi. Now, because we have used FOR EACH ROW, our trigger will execute 10 times, once for every customer being deleted. This is also called a Row-Level Trigger. In MySQL, triggers are row-level triggers, so FOR EACH ROW is required when creating them.\n\nOLD and NEW\n\nOLD and NEW are special references available inside triggers. They allow us to access the values of the row before and after an operation. Think of them as references to the row's previous and new values.\n\nOLD: Represents the existing row values before an operation.\nNEW: Represents the new row values being inserted or the updated values after an update.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
