    {
      "id": "w1epwoWo425GTP3RoITyw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2588.013203235066,
      "y": 4705.357312816082,
      "width": 1117.4792958349426,
      "height": 738.0077369439065,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b00",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 140733382,
      "version": 4311,
      "versionNonce": 1199012735,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "3tlxhjMfEgZUQwHEUrEzC",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678251254,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Think of it like a reusable function in Java or JavaScript, but it is designed\nspecifically for executing database operations.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to transfer money from one bank account to another. We\nneed to:\n1. Deduct money from the sender's account.\n2. Add money to the receiver's account.\n3. Make sure both operations succeed or neither takes effect.\nInstead of sending multiple queries separately from our backend, we can write a\nStored Procedure that handles all these operations.\n\nWe can execute it using: CALL transfer_money(1, 2, 500);\n\nHere, CALL executes the Stored Procedure.\n\nImportant: This example demonstrates the basic idea, but a real money-transfer\nprocedure must also validate the balance, handle errors, and use a transaction with\nrollback to prevent partial transfers.\n\nTypes of parameters in Stored Procedures\n\nWe can pass different types of parameters:\n\n- IN: Used to pass input into the procedure.\n- OUT: Used to return a value from the procedure.\n- INOUT: Used to pass a value into the procedure and modify it before returning it.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Think of it like a reusable function in Java or JavaScript, but it is designed specifically for executing database operations.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to transfer money from one bank account to another. We need to:\n1. Deduct money from the sender's account.\n2. Add money to the receiver's account.\n3. Make sure both operations succeed or neither takes effect.\nInstead of sending multiple queries separately from our backend, we can write a Stored Procedure that handles all these operations.\n\nWe can execute it using: CALL transfer_money(1, 2, 500);\n\nHere, CALL executes the Stored Procedure.\n\nImportant: This example demonstrates the basic idea, but a real money-transfer procedure must also validate the balance, handle errors, and use a transaction with rollback to prevent partial transfers.\n\nTypes of parameters in Stored Procedures\n\nWe can pass different types of parameters:\n\n- IN: Used to pass input into the procedure.\n- OUT: Used to return a value from the procedure.\n- INOUT: Used to pass a value into the procedure and modify it before returning it.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
