    {
      "id": "77Mk9aZ6knhpEQXgbgXyA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4377.582990532706,
      "y": 1289.8136619419984,
      "width": 1437.5875243732205,
      "height": 683.868085106383,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0R",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 268387020,
      "version": 5321,
      "versionNonce": 368141812,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "X4Yrx65M4lXDbsI9rd3rx",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794500609003,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We can create an index on a single column or on multiple columns of a table. When we create an index,\nSQL generally uses a B-Tree data structure to store the indexed values in a sorted manner, along with\nreferences to the corresponding rows. This helps SQL find records faster, especially when we use a\nWHERE clause to search for specific values.\n\nand CREATE INDEX idx_age ON users(age); This creates an index on age, which in databases like\nPostgreSQL and MySQL (InnoDB) is a non-clustered/secondary index.\n\nNow, suppose we use a WHERE clause with two conditions using AND, like WHERE age = 20 AND city =\n'Delhi'. If we have created a composite index (multi-column index) on both age and city, SQL can use\nthat index to search based on both columns. But what if we have created an index on only one column,\nsuch as age, and use both conditions in the WHERE clause? In that case, SQL can still use the index on\nage to find matching rows and then check the city condition on those rows. We don't necessarily need\nan index on both columns. Also, it doesn't matter which condition we write first in the WHERE clause.\nSQL's query optimizer decides which index to use based on the available indexes, data distribution,\nand estimated query cost. We can also create separate indexes on both columns, and SQL may choose to\nuse one or even combine both indexes, depending on the database and query.\n\n\nA clustered index determines the physical or logical order in which table records are stored,\ndepending on the database. Because a table can have only one physical ordering, it can generally have\nonly one clustered index.",
      "fontSize": 24.867930367504837,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We can create an index on a single column or on multiple columns of a table. When we create an index, SQL generally uses a B-Tree data structure to store the indexed values in a sorted manner, along with references to the corresponding rows. This helps SQL find records faster, especially when we use a WHERE clause to search for specific values.\n\nand CREATE INDEX idx_age ON users(age); This creates an index on age, which in databases like PostgreSQL and MySQL (InnoDB) is a non-clustered/secondary index.\n\nNow, suppose we use a WHERE clause with two conditions using AND, like WHERE age = 20 AND city = 'Delhi'. If we have created a composite index (multi-column index) on both age and city, SQL can use that index to search based on both columns. But what if we have created an index on only one column, such as age, and use both conditions in the WHERE clause? In that case, SQL can still use the index on age to find matching rows and then check the city condition on those rows. We don't necessarily need an index on both columns. Also, it doesn't matter which condition we write first in the WHERE clause. SQL's query optimizer decides which index to use based on the available indexes, data distribution, and estimated query cost. We can also create separate indexes on both columns, and SQL may choose to use one or even combine both indexes, depending on the database and query.\n\n\nA clustered index determines the physical or logical order in which table records are stored, depending on the database. Because a table can have only one physical ordering, it can generally have only one clustered index.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
