    {
      "id": "yzAfOaz8C2zaJTBAwHLlh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 329.3941556160171,
      "y": 4873.111281070051,
      "width": 1468.5904069460544,
      "height": 510.9284332688583,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0Z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1551337588,
      "version": 4674,
      "versionNonce": 2081100799,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "ZVeCIocNGPAU8MCgZP9a6",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678234062,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Cursors in stored procedures\nA cursor is used when we want to process the result of a SQL query one row at a time inside a stored procedure.\n\nNormally, when we execute a query like: SELECT * FROM employees;\n\nSQL returns multiple rows together as a result set. But sometimes we want to perform a separate operation on\neach returned row. This is where cursors are useful. Think of a cursor like an iterator in Java that moves\nthrough a collection one element at a time.\n\nExample: Suppose we have an employees table and want to process each employee's salary individually.\n\nLet's understand its execution:\n1. DECLARE CURSOR: We define a cursor using a SELECT query. It tells SQL which records we want to process.\n2. DECLARE HANDLER: We define what happens when the cursor has no more rows to fetch. Here, we set finished = 1.\n3. OPEN: We open the cursor so that we can start reading its result.\n4. FETCH: We fetch one row at a time and store its values in variables.\n5. LOOP: We repeat the fetching process until there are no more rows.\n6. CLOSE: Once all rows are processed, we close the cursor and release its resources.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Cursors in stored procedures\nA cursor is used when we want to process the result of a SQL query one row at a time inside a stored procedure.\n\nNormally, when we execute a query like: SELECT * FROM employees;\n\nSQL returns multiple rows together as a result set. But sometimes we want to perform a separate operation on each returned row. This is where cursors are useful. Think of a cursor like an iterator in Java that moves through a collection one element at a time.\n\nExample: Suppose we have an employees table and want to process each employee's salary individually.\n\nLet's understand its execution:\n1. DECLARE CURSOR: We define a cursor using a SELECT query. It tells SQL which records we want to process.\n2. DECLARE HANDLER: We define what happens when the cursor has no more rows to fetch. Here, we set finished = 1.\n3. OPEN: We open the cursor so that we can start reading its result.\n4. FETCH: We fetch one row at a time and store its values in variables.\n5. LOOP: We repeat the fetching process until there are no more rows.\n6. CLOSE: Once all rows are processed, we close the cursor and release its resources.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "I9_pF_rvQ9ruv_C5KwKRi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 567.3540433471862,
      "y": 6283.1112810700515,
      "width": 1468.5904069460544,
      "height": 908.3172147001926,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0ZG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 145386655,
      "version": 4596,
      "versionNonce": 1893099775,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "ZVeCIocNGPAU8MCgZP9a6",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678287388,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A window function performs calculations across a set of related rows without collapsing those rows into a single\nrow.\n\nThink of it this way:\n- GROUP BY combines multiple rows into groups and returns one row per group.\n- WINDOW FUNCTION performs calculations on groups of rows but keeps every original row in the result.\n\nLet's understand using your example. Suppose we have an employees table:\n\nWhy not just use GROUP BY?\nSuppose we want to calculate the total amount spent by each region. We can write:\n\nSELECT region, SUM(amount) AS region_total\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY region;\n\nHere, SQL groups all employees belonging to the same region and calculates their total amount. But there is a\nproblem! What if we also want to see each employee's name, individual amount, and the total amount of their\nregion?\nWith GROUP BY, we cannot simply include employee and amount in the SELECT statement because these columns are\nnot grouped or aggregated. This is where window functions become useful.\n\nUsing window functions We can write:\n\nSELECT *,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (\n           PARTITION BY region\n       ) AS region_total\nFROM employees;\n\nNotice something? Every employee still exists in the result, but we have an additional column containing the\ntotal amount of their region. This is the biggest advantage of window functions.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A window function performs calculations across a set of related rows without collapsing those rows into a single row.\n\nThink of it this way:\n- GROUP BY combines multiple rows into groups and returns one row per group.\n- WINDOW FUNCTION performs calculations on groups of rows but keeps every original row in the result.\n\nLet's understand using your example. Suppose we have an employees table:\n\nWhy not just use GROUP BY?\nSuppose we want to calculate the total amount spent by each region. We can write:\n\nSELECT region, SUM(amount) AS region_total\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY region;\n\nHere, SQL groups all employees belonging to the same region and calculates their total amount. But there is a problem! What if we also want to see each employee's name, individual amount, and the total amount of their region?\nWith GROUP BY, we cannot simply include employee and amount in the SELECT statement because these columns are not grouped or aggregated. This is where window functions become useful.\n\nUsing window functions We can write:\n\nSELECT *,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (\n           PARTITION BY region\n       ) AS region_total\nFROM employees;\n\nNotice something? Every employee still exists in the result, but we have an additional column containing the total amount of their region. This is the biggest advantage of window functions.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "y53b_mxN0237ynNjh7I7a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 578.969020080186,
      "y": 7837.777903875197,
      "width": 733.3650938069072,
      "height": 28.38491295938102,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0ZK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 577092337,
      "version": 4955,
      "versionNonce": 826208543,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "ZVeCIocNGPAU8MCgZP9a6",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678287388,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Understanding the syntax The general syntax is:",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Understanding the syntax The general syntax is:",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "SNTePjeWxxtplOZwHGMh1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 581.8461759369168,
      "y": 8493.657268055882,
      "width": 1733.2936006329546,
      "height": 482.54352030947734,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0ZM",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2017629841,
      "version": 5541,
      "versionNonce": 488126719,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "ZVeCIocNGPAU8MCgZP9a6",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794679503733,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A. PARTITION BY — Dividing rows into groups :- PARTITION BY divides the table into separate groups based on a column.\n\nB. ORDER BY — Defining the order inside each window :- Suppose we want to rank employees based on their amount within each region.\n\nSELECT employee, region, amount,\n       RANK() OVER (\n           PARTITION BY region\n           ORDER BY amount DESC\n       ) AS employee_rank\nFROM employees;\n\nPARTITION BY region creates separate windows for East, South and West.\nORDER BY amount DESC arranges employees from highest amount to lowest amount within each region.\nRANK() assigns a rank based on that ordering.\n\nThere are many more functions For example, SUM() calculates the total, AVG() calculates the average, RANK() assigns ranks, LAG()\naccesses the previous row's value, and LEAD() accesses the next row's value.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A. PARTITION BY — Dividing rows into groups :- PARTITION BY divides the table into separate groups based on a column.\n\nB. ORDER BY — Defining the order inside each window :- Suppose we want to rank employees based on their amount within each region.\n\nSELECT employee, region, amount,\n       RANK() OVER (\n           PARTITION BY region\n           ORDER BY amount DESC\n       ) AS employee_rank\nFROM employees;\n\nPARTITION BY region creates separate windows for East, South and West.\nORDER BY amount DESC arranges employees from highest amount to lowest amount within each region.\nRANK() assigns a rank based on that ordering.\n\nThere are many more functions For example, SUM() calculates the total, AVG() calculates the average, RANK() assigns ranks, LAG() accesses the previous row's value, and LEAD() accesses the next row's value.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "IHcm0OTQ46_fD_31VeSLB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 923.682249301658,
      "y": 8111.569011461769,
      "width": 2001.0403587021153,
      "height": 56.76982591876204,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0ZMV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1360373631,
      "version": 5670,
      "versionNonce": 1130263903,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "ZVeCIocNGPAU8MCgZP9a6",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1794678287388,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SUM(amount) tells SQL what calculation to perform.\nOVER() tells SQL to perform that calculation as a window function, considering a set of rows rather than collapsing everything into one result.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SUM(amount) tells SQL what calculation to perform.\nOVER() tells SQL to perform that calculation as a window function, considering a set of rows rather than collapsing everything into one result.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "J9Jk6pYupyGrD9WcjymoQ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 925.1041911166894,
      "y": 8228.794738773879,
      "width": 2001.0403587021153,
      "height": 56.76982591876204,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
